> `ERR_FAIL_COND_V(!p_dst && p_length > 0, -1);`

I do think this code might be worth refactoring to make it more obvious, as it's not the first time we're dealing with sanitizer reports or compiler warnings here, and overall it seems needlessly obtuse.

> This code wants to support writing to a null pointer with a 0 length.

This seems to be an expectation in the codebase indeed, which I believe mirrors `memcpy`?

But we can likely be a lot more obvious about it and just start with:
```cpp
if (p_length == 0) {
  return 0;
}
ERR_FAIL_COND_V(!p_dst, -1);
ERR_FAIL_NULL_V(data, -1);

...
```

Or am I missing something?

@bruvzg's suggestion also seems good, and combined with mine we can likely avoid the extra scope.

My main suggestion being: let's get the no-op case out of the way instead of making sure it can proceed through a bunch of statements it shouldn't run via convoluted `if` conditions.

